# Saving a response body to disk yourself, via a post-request script and
# require("fs") (same mechanism as 6_shell_auth.http's pre-request script).
#
# This is for cases the standard download mechanism ("# @download", see
# 9_binary_download.http) doesn't cover: saving a JSON/text *response body*
# for its own sake -- snapshotting an API response as a fixture, keeping a
# copy of something for later inspection, etc. -- rather than a server
# handing you a file it means for you to download (an image, a PDF, an
# export). For that latter case, use "# @download" instead: it's reliable
# for genuinely binary content, which this technique is not (see why
# below), and it doesn't need you to write any script at all.
#
# httpyac's JetBrains-style "@save" directive exists, but it's meant for
# an IDE's interactive "save as" dialog -- in this non-interactive CLI
# usage (what httpfly.nvim always uses) it silently does nothing, no file
# gets written. Scripting it yourself, as below, is the reliable way to
# save a *text* response body specifically.
#
# response.body's actual type depends on the response's Content-Type, and
# that matters for how you need to write it out:
#   - JSON        -> already-parsed object      -> JSON.stringify(...) first
#   - HTML/XML     -> a parsed DOM Document      -> not covered here
#   - anything else (plain text, csv, ...)       -> a plain string, write
#                                                    it directly
# Genuinely binary content (images, PDFs, zips, ...) can't be saved
# reliably this way at all: httpyac decodes the response body to a JS
# string somewhere in its own pipeline before scripts ever see it, and
# that's lossy for arbitrary bytes -- confirmed by trying to save a PNG
# this way and finding the saved file's first byte, and its overall size,
# no longer matched the original (a valid PNG starts with byte 0x89; the
# written file didn't, and was hundreds of bytes shorter than the
# original Content-Length). "# @download" (9_binary_download.http) exists
# specifically because of this limitation.
#
# Run `make httpbin-up` from the repo root first (requires Docker).

###
# @name SaveJsonResponse
GET http://localhost:8080/get?report=example HTTP/1.1

> {%
  const fs = require("fs");
  fs.writeFileSync("/tmp/httpfly-saved-response.json", JSON.stringify(response.body, null, 2), "utf8");
%}

###
# @name SaveTextResponse
GET http://localhost:8080/robots.txt HTTP/1.1

> {%
  require("fs").writeFileSync("/tmp/httpfly-saved-response.txt", response.body, "utf8");
  client.test("Downloaded to /tmp/httpfly-saved-response.txt", () => true)
%}
